The Semantic Graph Vision
This is the soul of Astra. Everything else is features. This is the foundation.

What It Actually Is
Imagine your entire codebase as a living city.
Functions        →  buildings
Dependencies     →  roads between buildings
Data flow        →  traffic on those roads
Git history      →  the city's historical records
Team knowledge   →  who built which neighbourhood
PR descriptions  →  the architect's notes
Comments         →  signs on the buildings
Every other tool sees individual files. Astra sees the entire city.

What It Stores
NODES (things that exist):
→ Every function, class, type, interface
→ Every file, module, service
→ Every developer who touched each node
→ Every commit that changed each node
→ Every PR that discussed each node
→ Every comment and TODO inside each node

EDGES (relationships between things):
→ This function calls that function
→ This type depends on that type
→ This service sends data to that service
→ This file was always changed alongside that file
→ This bug was introduced when this node changed
→ This developer understands this cluster best

What Makes It Alive
Most code graphs are static snapshots. They show you what exists right now.
Astra's graph is temporal. It has a fourth dimension — time.
Static graph:    UserService → calls → AuthService
Astra's graph:   UserService → calls → AuthService
                 [but 3 months ago it called OldAuthService]
                 [the switch happened in PR #47]
                 [because of a security incident]
                 [and @sarah made that decision]
                 [and there are still 2 places using the old path]
That last line. That's the one that saves you from a production incident.

What It Enables
Every single Astra feature is just a different query on this graph:
Time Travel Debug   →  "find the node that changed when this broke"
Archaeology         →  "show me the full history of this node"
Security Hunter     →  "find nodes that touch user input and reach the DB"
Onboarding          →  "show a junior the most important nodes first"
Watchdog            →  "alert when a critical node changes unexpectedly"
Cross-repo          →  "find equivalent nodes in another codebase"
Predictive          →  "which nodes change together most often"
Health Dashboard    →  "score the overall graph health"
Migration           →  "map nodes from Python to Go equivalents"
The graph isn't a feature. It's the engine everything runs on.

How It Grows
Day 1:    Basic graph — functions, files, dependencies
Week 1:   Git history added — every node has a timeline
Month 1:  Team patterns emerge — who owns what
Month 3:  Predictive patterns — what always breaks together
Month 6:  The graph knows your codebase better than your team
Year 1:   Institutional knowledge that survives team turnover
That last one is the multi-million dollar value proposition for enterprises. When a senior engineer leaves — their knowledge usually leaves with them. With Astra's graph it stays forever.

The One Demo That Sells It
$ astra why src/payments/processor.rs

▸ This file was born on Jan 14 from a 3-way merge conflict
▸ It's been touched by 6 developers — only @mike fully understands it
▸ It has 3 layers of logic added at different times:
  → Core payment flow (Jan) — @mike
  → Retry logic (March) — @sarah, after the Stripe outage incident  
  → Fraud detection hooks (July) — @you, 2 days before your deadline
▸ The retry logic and fraud hooks have never been tested together
▸ There's a TODO on line 234 that's been there 8 months
▸ This file is changed every time /api/checkout changes
  → suggesting hidden coupling that isn't in the imports
▸ Prediction: the next change here will introduce a bug
   confidence: 73%
Nobody. Has. This.
Not OpenCode. Not Devin. Not Claude Code. Not GitHub Copilot. Not SonarQube. Not Linear. Nobody.
That's the semantic graph. That's Astra's soul. 🎯
The semantic memory system is different from the graph. They work together but they're not the same thing.

The Difference First
Semantic Graph   →  understands STRUCTURE
                    "how does this codebase connect"

Semantic Memory  →  understands HISTORY
                    "what happened, when, and why"
The graph is a map of your city. The memory is the city's diary.

What The Semantic Memory Actually Is
Most "memory" in AI tools is just storing your chat history. That's not memory — that's a log file.
Astra's semantic memory is fundamentally different. It stores meaning not just data.
Normal memory:    "user asked about auth on March 14"
Semantic memory:  "the auth system is understood to be fragile
                   because of 3 incidents, 2 rewrites, and 1 near-miss
                   that was never documented anywhere"
That second one came from reading 847 commits, 23 PRs, 156 comments, and 6 months of git history. No human told Astra that. It figured it out.

The Four Layers Of Memory
Layer 1 — Episodic Memory
What actually happened, in order, with timestamps:
March 14  →  auth middleware rewritten after JWT exploit
March 28  →  retry logic added after Stripe outage
April 2   →  @sarah left the team
April 3   →  3 PRs touching auth merged same day (unusual)
April 15  →  first production incident since rewrite
This is the raw timeline. Every event that mattered.

Layer 2 — Semantic Memory
What those events mean extracted and stored as concepts:
CONCEPT: "auth system is high risk"
EVIDENCE: 2 rewrites, 1 exploit, 1 incident, 1 near-miss
CONFIDENCE: 91%
LAST UPDATED: 6 days ago

CONCEPT: "payments and auth are secretly coupled"
EVIDENCE: changed together 14 times without explicit imports
CONFIDENCE: 87%
LAST UPDATED: 2 weeks ago

CONCEPT: "@mike is the only person who understands processor.rs"
EVIDENCE: 73% of meaningful commits, only one who catches its bugs
CONFIDENCE: 95%
LAST UPDATED: yesterday
These concepts didn't exist in any file. Astra derived them.

Layer 3 — Procedural Memory
How YOUR team actually works — not how they say they work:
YOUR TEAM'S ACTUAL PATTERNS:
→ Friday commits are 3x more likely to cause Monday incidents
→ PRs over 400 lines are never properly reviewed
→ Auth changes always need 2 days of monitoring after deploy
→ @ciphr works best on greenfield — struggles with legacy debt
→ Test coverage drops every sprint before a deadline
Nobody documented these. Astra observed them.

Layer 4 — Prospective Memory
What Astra is watching for based on everything it knows:
WATCHING:
→ processor.rs hasn't been touched in 47 days
   but checkout.ts changes daily — drift building up
   
→ @mike hasn't committed in 3 weeks
   if he leaves, 4 critical files become orphaned

→ The fraud detection hook has never been tested
   under the retry logic path — ticking time bomb
This is memory that lives in the future. Not what happened — what's about to happen.

How It Works Technically
Every commit        →  memory system reads the diff
                        extracts concepts
                        updates confidence scores
                        connects to existing memories
                        checks prospective watches

Every question      →  memory system finds relevant concepts
                        ranks by confidence and recency
                        provides context to the AI
                        stores the question as episodic memory

Every incident      →  memory system updates risk scores
                        strengthens related concepts
                        adds to procedural patterns
It's not a database. It's not a vector store. It's a living knowledge system that gets smarter every single day you use it.

The Moment It Becomes Irreplaceable
Six months in, a new developer joins your team. They ask Astra:
$ astra "where should I start to understand this codebase"

▸ Based on 6 months of watching this codebase here's what matters:

  Start here:
  1. /core/auth — highest risk, most history, most impactful
  2. /services/payments — secretly coupled to everything
  3. /workers/sync — least documented, most confusing

  Avoid for now:
  → /legacy/* — active rewrite in progress, don't touch
  → /experimental/* — abandoned, nobody knows what it does

  People to talk to:
  → @mike for payments (but he's been away 3 weeks — check in)
  → @sarah for auth (she left but her PRs explain everything)
  → @ciphr for anything new — don't ask about the legacy worker

  Things that will surprise you:
  → The "users" service is actually the auth service (historical naming)
  → Friday deploys always cause Monday incidents — team knows but ignores
  → There's a bug in the retry logic nobody has fixed for 4 months
    open issue #234 — ask @ciphr why it's still open
That answer took 0.3 seconds. It would take a new developer 3 weeks to learn all of that organically.
That's the semantic memory system. 🎯

How It Connects To The Graph
Semantic Graph   →  tells you HOW the codebase is connected
Semantic Memory  →  tells you WHY it got that way

Together:        →  complete institutional knowledge
                    that survives team changes forever
One without the other is incomplete. Together they're unprecedented.
No tool has both. Astra does. That's the moat. 🚀